WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.020
How do you put a chosen gene inside a living cell?

2
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.100
A gene alone cannot simply cross a cell membrane.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:14.120
Instead, biologists use engineered viruses, called viral vectors, to carry chosen

4
00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:15.600
genes into target cells.

5
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:24.840
Researchers pack a chosen gene into an engineered virus, such as a retrovirus or an

6
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.200
adeno-associated virus.

7
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:30.740
The vector enters the target cell and delivers that gene.

8
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.540
The cell then builds the new protein, such as a missing channel or a light-gated

9
00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:37.540
channel.

10
00:00:38.083 --> 00:00:40.283
Not every gene fits in one shell.

11
00:00:41.543 --> 00:00:47.983
A conventional adeno-associated virus, or AAV, carries about 4.7 to 5 kb.

12
00:00:49.343 --> 00:00:51.763
A kilobase is a thousand DNA letters.

13
00:00:53.223 --> 00:00:58.643
Otoferlin's coding sequence is about 6 kb before a promoter switch is added.

14
00:00:59.843 --> 00:01:04.743
So two vectors carry separate parts, and cells receiving both rejoin the full gene.

15
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:07.900
Splitting a gene carries a cost.

16
00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:14.280
Suppose each of two vectors independently reaches 70% % of inner hair cells.

17
00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.840
What fraction of those cells can make full-length otoferlin?

18
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:21.560
Pause and find the overlap.

19
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:31.753
Because both deliveries are independent, multiply the two fractions.

20
00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:45.553
Even at 70% % per vector, fewer than half the cells build full-length otoferlin.

21
00:01:46.933 --> 00:01:52.313
Viral vectors deliver chosen genes into living cells, but size and controls bound

22
00:01:54.217 --> 00:01:57.157
Getting a gene inside is only half the test.

23
00:01:58.457 --> 00:02:03.577
Restoring a lost current, called rescue, still needs vector and expression controls

24
00:02:03.577 --> 00:02:05.197
to rule out delivery effects.

25
00:02:06.577 --> 00:02:10.837
Even then, rescue cannot tell a channel pore from an overexpressed substitute.

26
00:02:12.197 --> 00:02:16.017
And preserving cones in animal models is still not a human trial.

27
00:02:17.083 --> 00:02:21.383
With tight controls, viral vectors unlocked three landmark results.

28
00:02:22.643 --> 00:02:28.963
In 2006, Kazutoshi Takahashi and Shinya Yamanaka used retroviruses on mouse

29
00:02:28.963 --> 00:02:31.763
connective-tissue cells, called fibroblasts.

30
00:02:33.123 --> 00:02:38.963
Delivering 24 stem-cell genes, then omitting one at a time, left four gene-switching

31
00:02:38.963 --> 00:02:40.083
transcription factors.

32
00:02:40.083 --> 00:02:46.543
Those four turned mouse fibroblasts into induced pluripotent stem cells, or iPS

33
00:02:46.543 --> 00:02:47.543
cells.

34
00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:51.613
In 2018, Pan and colleagues probed a working channel.

35
00:02:52.893 --> 00:02:58.513
A vector delivered altered TMC1 into mutant mouse hair cells, restoring current.

36
00:02:59.853 --> 00:03:03.073
Each copy carried one added cysteine, an amino acid.

37
00:03:04.293 --> 00:03:08.713
A reagent that cannot cross the membrane grabs cysteine where water reaches it.

38
00:03:09.833 --> 00:03:14.553
When it altered ion flow in working cells, neither synthesis nor delivery had

39
00:03:14.553 --> 00:03:15.553
changed.

40
00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:21.310
In 2019, Al-Moyed and colleagues worked around the size limit for otoferlin.

41
00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:26.430
They split its coding sequence between two AAV2/6 vectors.

42
00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:31.130
Cells receiving both joined the parts back into the full-length gene.

43
00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:37.930
From stem cells to hearing channels, engineered viruses answer questions only within

44
00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:39.390
cargo and control limits.
